      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the side length of the square region:**\n\n   Let the side length of the square region be \\( s \\). Since we are using whole 2-by-3 rectangles, the side length \\( s \\) must be a multiple of both 2 and 3. The smallest \\( s \\) that satisfies this condition is 6.\n\n   \\[\n   s = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of 2-by-3 rectangles that can fit along one side of the square:**\n\n   Along the side of length 6, we can fit:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{s}{2} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{6}{2} \\right\\rfloor = 3\n   \\]\n   rectangles along the length direction.\n\n   Along the side of length 6, we can fit:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor \\frac{s}{3} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{6}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 2\n   \\]\n   rectangles along the width direction.\n\n3. **Calculate the total number of 2-by-3 rectangles:**\n\n   The total number of 2-by-3 rectangles that can fit into the 2-by-3 grid is:\n   \\[\n   3 \\times 2 = 6\n   \\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a 6-by-3 square region exactly is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
