      "text": "To determine the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles that can cover a square region exactly, we need to consider the areas and properties of the rectangles. Let's start by calculating the area of the square region and the area of one rectangle.\n\nIf the side length of the square is \\( n \\), then the area of the square is \\( n^2 \\). The area of one 2-by-3 rectangle is \\( 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\). Therefore, \\( n^2 \\) must be divisible by 6. The smallest number \\( n \\) that satisfies this condition is \\( n = 6 \\) because \\( 6^2 = 36 \\) and 36 is divisible by 6.\n\nNext, we need to check if it is possible to cover a 6-by-6 square exactly with 6-by-3 rectangles. We can try to tile the square using these rectangles. Let's start by placing the rectangles in a 2-by-3 grid pattern. We can place 2 rectangles in each 2-by-3 grid:\n\\[ \\begin{array}{|||} \\hline \\bullet & \\bullet \\cdot & & \\\\ \\hline \\cdot & \\bullet & \\bullet & \\cdot \\cdot \\cdot \\cdot \\cdot \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\]\nThe 6-by-6 square can be completely covered by these 2-by-3 rectangles.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a 6-by-6 square exactly is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
